  {
    "episode_id": 93179,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/9/3/93179.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 50,
    "turn_text": "So for me, I visualize what I'm doing and when I feel really scared, I think of skydiving. And so I'm feeling really scared and you feel all of that adrenaline and it's like, you know what? I'm just going to jump out of the airplane. And you just think of it like that. Think of it as skydiving and not just jumping off a cliff. But there is a parachute. There is a parachute. You have to work on your parachute. Your parachute is self-love. Your parachute is learning how to forgive yourself. Your parachute is saying, you know what? That's OK. Your parachute is calling up your best friend and just saying, screw the world. Let's have some Chinese food and watch Netflix. And I think about my parachute all the time. And I'm very lucky to have really strong friendships and friends and family that love me no matter what. And even now, to this day, I'm like, OK, what is the worst thing that could happen in this situation, right? Worst thing that could happen to me is that I'm a one-term congresswoman, like that I don't get reelected or they gerrymandered me out of my district or whatever it is. And you know what? I sometimes visualize it. And I was like, we joke that. We're just going to move up state and build a yurt and just live off the land. And so that's my worst case scenario. And it's not that that's what I'm trying to do, but that's what's going to happen. But you think what's the worst that could happen? And the worst that could happen is that I'm just going to try to lead a normal life.",
