  },
  {
    "episode_id": 93179,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/9/3/93179.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 57,
    "turn_text": "I think the first thing you need to do is identify the tiniest step, like Rashma said. It could just be buying the URL. It could just be sketching in your notebook. One of the things that I was thinking about doing was doing children's books that reflect our community. And that was one of my first projects. I wanted to work on children's books where young girls of color were the protagonists and it wasn't just a book about You know, sweet valley high exactly exactly like I wanted it I wanted it to just be this epic story and the protagonist was us and it was in our home and in our environment and My perfectionism is it was really that perfectionism that was my enemy. It's like oh well. It's not It's not perfect. It's the worst. And I would just stop trying. And so one of the concepts that we started to learn about was just having a nonzero day. And a nonzero day was like, did you do one tiny thing? Did you buy a URL? Did you have a little sketch? Did you send that email to just march something forward? And don't compare your present state to what you want the final vision in your head to be. Don't be like, oh, I'm so far away from that thing. I'm failing. Just think of success as did I do something to move it forward today. And even if it's that tiny, tiny little movement, it's still really, really important. And you're advancing a lot further than you think you are when you take that tiny little step. That's awesome.",
    "speaker_id": "SPEAKER_03",
    "inferred_speaker_name": null,
    "inferred_speaker_role": null,
    "chosen_text_key": "transcript",
