  {
    "episode_id": 93179,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/9/3/93179.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 68,
    "turn_text": "So there's a lot. One that I think we have control of right now is this idea that you can't be what you can't see. And so I felt like for a very long time, one of the biggest barriers to leadership is I know when I was growing up, I didn't see any women like me in positions of leadership. And so when you're only seeing white dudes just running the world, you think you need to act like a white dude to run the world. Straight up, you think you need to talk a certain way, dress a certain way, have your shoulder pads out to here, be like, hi, jam. You think that's how you have to move through the world. And I found myself subconsciously doing that in high school and in college. I felt like I needed to write a certain way and talk a certain way. And like the things that they like and play golf or all that stuff. And the problem is that that mold wasn't made for you. And so even if you try the hardest at being that, you will not be as good as someone who's just that already. And so that's why I've tried really hard to authentically be myself while I'm here in this moment and in this position because I want to show other people that there are other ways of being powerful in the world. Because one of the biggest barriers that we have is like you know, even when you look at coding or tech, there's this idea that you have to like be this like bro. and that you have to not care about how people feel, or not care about community, or just be like, I'm a coder, I don't care about emotions. Who",
    "speaker_id": "SPEAKER_03",
    "inferred_speaker_name": null,
    "inferred_speaker_role": null,
    "chosen_text_key": "transcript",
    "explicit_propositions": [
      {
        "text": "One barrier to leadership is the lack of visible role models who resemble oneself.",
        "confidence": 0.96
      },
      {
        "text": "When individuals only see white men in leadership roles, they may believe they must emulate those traits to succeed.",
        "confidence": 0.94
      },
      {
        "text": "The speaker internalized behaviors associated with dominant leadership stereotypes during high school and college.",
        "confidence": 0.92
      },
      {
        "text": "The speaker believed they had to adopt specific speech patterns, clothing styles, and interests to fit into leadership roles.",
        "confidence": 0.91
      },
      {
        "text": "The standard leadership archetype is not designed for people outside the dominant demographic group.",
        "confidence": 0.9
      },
      {
        "text": "Attempting to conform to the dominant leadership mold leads to inferior performance compared to natural fits.",
        "confidence": 0.89
      },
      {
        "text": "Authenticity in self-expression is more effective and sustainable than mimicry in leadership.",
        "confidence": 0.88
      },
      {
        "text": "Visible representation of diverse leaders helps others envision alternative pathways to power.",
        "confidence": 0.87
      },
      {
        "text": "In tech fields, there is a prevalent cultural stereotype equating masculinity with technical competence.",
        "confidence": 0.86
      }
    ],
    "assumptions": [
      {
        "text": "Leadership effectiveness should be measured by authenticity rather than conformity to norms.",
        "confidence": 0.93
